## Evening Session: Process vs. Thread

**Explanation:**

A process is a running instance of a program that has its own private, isolated memory space — the operating system does not 
allow one process to directly read or write another process's memory. A thread, on the other hand, is a smaller unit of execution 
that exists inside a process. A single process can have multiple threads, and all of those threads share the same memory space as 
their parent process, meaning they can access the same data directly. The key difference is isolation: processes are walled off from 
each other, while threads within the same process are not — they share resources and can communicate with each other much more easily 
and efficiently than two separate processes can.

Looking at my own Task Manager screenshot, this is visible directly: "Google Chrome (11)" is a single process grouping together 11 
tabs/extensions that likely run as separate lightweight processes or threads, each isolated enough that one crashing tab doesn't 
take down the whole browser — while still sharing overall memory allocated to Chrome as a whole.

**Real-world analogy:**

A process is like a sealed office with its own filing cabinet, desk, and equipment — nothing inside that office is visible or accessible 
to people in other offices. A thread is like an employee working inside that office. One office can have several employees (threads) 
working at once, and they all share the same filing cabinet and desk (memory) — one employee can hand a document directly to another 
without leaving the room. But if an employee from a different office wants that same document, they'd have to go through a formal process 
(like inter-process communication) rather than just reaching over and grabbing it.

